description_details:
Motivation: We observed two customer incidents where streaming responses contained truncated JSON or duplicated function-call sentinels after intermittent network re-fragmentation. Those failures primarily affected the OpenAI compatibility layer where clients assume each 'data: ' block corresponds to a discrete semantic event. Network-level fragmentation and aggressive kernel coalescing could interleave halves of different events in a single read, and our previous reassembly strategy relied on naive newline-splitting which is brittle. Changes: - Introduce optional chunk metadata: when generating SSE we now include an inline JSON prefix when needed, e.g. 'data: {"chunk_seq":123,"boundary":false}...
' followed by the payload. Parsers only use this when present (backwards compatible). - Reassembly window: buffer up to N events or M bytes (configurable, defaults: 64 events / 256KB) and use chunk_seq with a small gap-tolerant algorithm to order fragments; if sequence gaps exceed threshold the reassembler emits partial best-effort output and logs an event for postmortem. - Boundary sentinel: when an event is complete we append a single-line sentinel 'data: {"boundary":true}

' which downstream can use to flush event assembly deterministically. - Robust newline handling: normalize CRLF/LF and handle 'data: ' repeats per SSE spec while avoiding accidental JSON concatenation. - Tests and docs: added unit/integration tests that simulate TCP-level splits and included a new doc section describing the optional markers and how clients should behave.
